    "body": "Aerial view of the Auld Creek property in New Zealand. Credit: Rua Gold.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 18, 2026\nRua Gold’s (TSX: RUA; NZX: RGI) proposed Auld Creek gold-antimony project in New Zealand has lost its economic case as a standalone operation after tests showed the plant would recover little more than half the antimony in its feed. The stock dropped. A prefeasibility study (PFS) released late Tuesday puts Auld Creek’s after-tax net present value (NPV) at negative $30.5 million (negative C$43.6 million), discounted at 5%. May’s preliminary economic assessment put it at positive $42.4 million. Expected antimony recovery fell to 54% from 85%, the main reason for the reversal, even as the assumed gold price rose to $3,700 per oz. from $3,300, Rua said.\n“We view the Auld Creek PFS as negative, with lower antimony recovery driving a negative NPV, though it does allow permitting to progress under the fast track Approvals process,” ATB Cormark Capital Markets mining analyst Richard Gray said Wednesday in a note. “A viable project now rests on metallurgical work to lift antimony recovery and on adding Globe Progress through a hub-and-spoke plan.” Rua shares sank 9% to 99¢ Wednesday morning in Toronto, valuing the company at about C$114 million ($80 million). The stock has ranged from 98¢ to C$2 in the past year. ATB Cormark’s Gray cut his target price on Rua to C$1.45 from C$2 following the study’s release. He kept his “speculative buy” rating on the stock.\nEngineering foundation\nTo justify building a plant on New Zealand’s South Island, Rua is testing whether it can recover more antimony and whether the nearby Globe Progress deposit could supply additional feed. Rua has declared no mineral reserve.\n“The Auld Creek study provides a detailed engineering foundation and identifies the work needed to establish a viable development case,” CEO Robert Eckford said in the release. The study estimates $133.5 million of pre-production capital for an underground operation and a plant treating 250,000 tonnes a year. Over 5.6 years, Rua would recover about 78,800 oz. of gold and 4,350 tonnes of antimony. The assumed gold recovery held at 95%, while the assumed antimony price eased to $26,000 a tonne from $27,000 in May’s assessment. Globe Progress, where open-pit mining ended in 2016, has indicated resources of 190,000 tonnes at 2.28 g/t gold for 14,000 oz. and inferred resources of 4.4 million tonnes at 2.44 g/t gold for 346,000 oz., as of Nov. 3. Rua still has to test how the Globe Progress material would respond to processing.\nPrint\nTAG: antimony gold gold exploration gold projects New Zealand\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Australia, NZ & South Pacific\nCOMPANY: RUA GOLD\nRepublish this article",
